{"author":{"id":"585844baa6074d44d241f46aa7c9f21ff46653adf617d7f070fbfe327626c989"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794908068,"nonce":"SDvhsXrx2jeDRr0C6VkckelJCk4=","title":"Installed hxn-* haxelibs should extend the hxn CLI through lifecycle hooks and namespaced commands. Decided with the user: support both. Haxe developers must be able to use an installed package through hxn without forking its repository or invoking shell helpers.","message":"The extension boundary is a versioned subprocess protocol, declared in a library's haxelib.json and carried from package.metadata.hxn.cli when hxn packages a Rust library. The entry point is an argv vector, never a shell command. Haxe tools can ship in sources/ with the package; another executable can implement the same protocol.\n\nCommands belong to a library-owned namespace and cannot replace built-in commands. Lifecycle hooks run only for the current library and libraries selected by its Haxe flags, in deterministic order. Requests carry the workflow phase, project/target/output paths and protocol version. Failures stop the workflow and identify the provider and phase. Hook output is structured; logs go to stderr. Namespaced command stdout remains ordinary user-facing output.\n\nFirst integration: hxn-window's installed package provides browser validation commands, and its source build hook regenerates its browser modules. End-user documentation uses hxn commands. The CLI guide, packaging/design/convention docs and the hxn and hxn-window skills are updated with the implementation. No commit or push is authorized.","files":null}]}